### How do you filter a map by value?

A map has no stream() method. You call entrySet().stream() to get a stream of Map.Entry objects, filter on the value, and collect back into a map.

```java
package com.bank.products;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FilterMapByValue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> productPrices = Map.of(
            "Savings Account",  0,
            "Current Account",  50,
            "Premium Account", 200,
            "Gold Locker",     150,
            "Basic Locker",     75
        );

        Map<String, Integer> premium = productPrices.entrySet().stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getValue() > 100)
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                Map.Entry::getKey,
                Map.Entry::getValue));

        System.out.println("Premium products: " + premium);
    }
}
```

```text
Premium products: {Premium Account=200, Gold Locker=150}
```

### Can you filter on both key and value?

Yes. The predicate has access to the full entry.

```java
// Keep lockers priced above 100
Map<String, Integer> expensiveLockers = productPrices.entrySet().stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getKey().contains("Locker")
        && e.getValue() > 100)
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Map.Entry::getKey, Map.Entry::getValue));
```

### How do you filter in place instead of creating a new map?

```java
// In-place removal: modifies the original map
Map<String, Integer> mutable = new HashMap<>(productPrices);
mutable.entrySet().removeIf(e -> e.getValue() <= 100);
System.out.println(mutable);
// {Premium Account=200, Gold Locker=150}
```

| Approach | Original map | Creates new map? |
|---|---|---|
| stream + filter + toMap | Unchanged | Yes |
| entrySet().removeIf() | Modified | No |

### How do you preserve insertion order in the result?

```java
Map<String, Integer> ordered = productPrices.entrySet().stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getValue() > 100)
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Map.Entry::getKey,
        Map.Entry::getValue,
        (a, b) -> a,
        LinkedHashMap::new));
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: calling stream() on the map directly.

```java
// WRONG: Map has no stream() method
productPrices.stream()  // compile error!

// CORRECT: stream the entrySet
productPrices.entrySet().stream()
```

Mistake: forgetting that > 100 excludes exactly 100.

```java
// This excludes products priced at exactly 100
filter(e -> e.getValue() > 100)

// This includes products priced at exactly 100
filter(e -> e.getValue() >= 100)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Keys in the source map are unique, so the rebuilt toMap needs no merge function.
- Choosing between a new map and in-place removal is a decision about immutability and whether the source should change.
- For concurrent maps, use ConcurrentHashMap.entrySet().removeIf() for thread-safe in-place filtering.
- The filter predicate is a Predicate<Map.Entry>, so you can compose it with and(), or(), negate().

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you filter a map by key?" Same pattern: filter(e -> e.getKey().startsWith("Premium")).
- "How do you count how many entries survive?" Use count() instead of collect.
- "How do you get just the keys or values after filtering?" Collect into a List with mapping.
